>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `enable_split_attribute`

``` hol4
splitLib.enable_split_attribute : unit -> unit
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Enables the persistent `[split]` theorem attribute.

After `enable_split_attribute ()`, a theorem declared with the `split`
attribute (`Theorem name[split]: ...`) is recorded as a split rule. The
rules are exported with the theory, used by `simpLib.split_ss`, and
returned by `splitLib.split_thms` and `splitLib.named_split_thms`. Before
the attribute is enabled, these return no rules. Split rules passed
directly (`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`, `simpLib.Split`, `simpLib.add_split`)
and datatype case splitting do not need the attribute.

Calling the function again has no effect.

### Failure

Fails if a `split` attribute or theorem set has already been registered by
other code.

### Comments

A theory's `[split]` rules are only loaded when the attribute is already
enabled when the theory is loaded. A script declaring `[split]` rules
should therefore use the library `splitPersistLib`, which enables the
attribute when it is loaded, and make it a dependency of the exported
theory:

``` hol4
Theory mySplits
Libs
  splitLib splitPersistLib

val _ = Theory.add_ML_dependency "splitPersistLib"
```

This is needed in the earliest theory declaring `[split]` rules;
descendant theories then see the rules, and their retraction by
`splitLib.exclude_split`. After `Context.restore` to a point before the
attribute was enabled, call `enable_split_attribute ()` again.

### See also

[`splitLib.exclude_split`](#splitLib.exclude_split),
[`simpLib.split_ss`](#simpLib.split_ss),
[`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC),
[`simpLib.Split`](#simpLib.Split).
